WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.856
Lifetimes and the break-even.

2
00:00:02.136 --> 00:00:07.875
The default cache entry lives for five minutes, and every read refreshes it at no extra cost.

3
00:00:08.155 --> 00:00:11.867
A one-hour lifetime is available with "ttl": "1h".

4
00:00:12.147 --> 00:00:18.995
The lifetime is measured from the start of the request that writes or reads the entry, so a long generation eats into it.

5
00:00:19.275 --> 00:00:21.664
The prices make the decision arithmetic.

6
00:00:21.944 --> 00:00:24.376
On Claude, a five-minute write costs 1.

7
00:00:24.656 --> 00:00:29.243
25 times the base input price and a one-hour write costs 2 times.

8
00:00:29.523 --> 00:00:30.888
A read costs 0.

9
00:00:31.168 --> 00:00:33.600
05 times base on Opus 5.

10
00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:35.373
5 and Sonnet 5.

11
00:00:35.653 --> 00:00:36.763
5 and 0.

12
00:00:37.043 --> 00:00:40.221
1 times on most other models, at the time of writing.

13
00:00:40.501 --> 00:00:44.576
So a five-minute entry pays for itself the first time it is read (1.

14
00:00:44.856 --> 00:00:50.701
25 plus a read is less than 2 full-price sends), and a one-hour entry needs two reads.

15
00:00:50.981 --> 00:00:57.061
Which lifetime to pick depends on the gaps between requests that share a prefix, not on their average.

16
00:00:57.341 --> 00:01:08.435
If requests start less than five minutes apart, each one refreshes the five-minute entry and it stays warm indefinitely, so the one-hour lifetime buys nothing but a pricier write.

17
00:01:08.715 --> 00:01:19.957
If gaps of five to sixty minutes are common (a user who replies after twenty minutes, a batch job that takes longer than five minutes), the one-hour entry avoids repeated writes.

18
00:01:20.237 --> 00:01:29.176
Anthropic’s cost guide offers a rule of thumb: prefer the one-hour lifetime when about one gap in twenty falls between five minutes and an hour.

19
00:01:29.456 --> 00:01:34.533
Beyond an hour, neither helps; you either re-warm on a schedule or accept the cold miss.

20
00:01:34.813 --> 00:01:38.824
Caching also helps latency, though less than cost on short prompts.

21
00:01:39.104 --> 00:01:57.195
Anthropic’s latency cookbook measured caching cutting cost per task by 85 percent on its sample agent with no significant change in wait, while on a two-turn conversation with a 146,000-token prompt the second turn’s wait fell from 1.

22
00:01:57.475 --> 00:01:58.989
7 seconds to 0.

23
00:01:59.269 --> 00:02:00.037
5.

24
00:02:00.317 --> 00:02:03.432
Long prefixes are where caching shows up in seconds.
